  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 305 AM MDT Wed Sep 23 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA mid-level shortwave disturbance and its associated moisture will \ncontinue to track northeast across eastern MT today with some \nlingering showers and cloud-cover still this morning across \neastern portions of central and southwest MT before clouds decrease\nthis afternoon. Unseasonably warm temperatures persist through \nFriday with warmest readings still on track for Thursday and \nFriday as southwesterly mid-level flow increases ahead of an \nincoming large scale trough from the northern Pacific. Gusty south\nto southwest winds develop late Friday ahead of an approaching \ncold front with the incoming upper level trough, with a few \nshowers and possibly some thunderstorms as the front passes Friday\nevening/overnight. Windy and cooler conditions follow on Saturday\nwith locally stronger winds expected along areas adjacent to the \nRocky Mountain Front. A faster zonal (W-E) progression of weather \nsystems looks to follow through early next week, maintaining \ntemperatures closer to or even slightly below seasonal averages \nwhile embedded disturbances bring some chances for precipitation \nwith low confidence in timing or extent of moisture still at this \npoint. \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nWind continues to be the main concern with the approach and passage \nof the frontal system late Friday and Friday night as well as in the \nfast westerly flow that follows late Friday night through early \nSaturday. The Friday evening arrival of the Pacific cold front may \nmeet a very warm/dry and somewhat unstable airmass still in place \nahead of the front east of the Rockies, which could support enhanced \nfrontal winds as the front sweeps east across north-central MT, \nespecially if any convection (showers/isolated thunderstorms) \ndevelop in association with the frontal passage. Strong pressure \nrises are also indicated which should contribute to gusts exceeding \n40-50 mph following the front across north-central MT Friday night. \nMountain wave enhancement will likely also play a role later Friday \nnight through early Saturday with strongest winds along the Rocky \nMtn Front and a 30-50% probability for gusts higher than 55 mph as \nfar east as Cut Bank and Great Falls. Hoenisch",
